//Projects/PEX/List/README.txt

# Microsoft PEX (BDD)
Microsoft Pex is a unit test framework for .NET that can effectively create a large set of tests to exercise a code under specification-by-example. 

## List Example

**List Class Design**
A List class should contain the following:

    - Add(int item): Adds 'item' at the end of the list
    - Remove(int item): Removes the first occurrence of 'item' from the list
    - RemoveAt(int index): Removes the item at the given index
    - Insert(int index, int item): Inserts 'item' at the given index
    - Contains(int item): Checks if the list contains 'item'
    - Count: The number of elements in the list
    - Clear(): Removes all the elements from the list
    - this[int index]: Accessor to the element at the given index

**Test Cases**
We are going to test each public method according to the different scenarios/cases it should handle.

```C#
public void PUT_AddContract(List<int> list, int x) {...}
public void PUT_RemoveContract(List<int> list, int x) {...}
public void PUT_--------------------

        }
    }

    }

}

//Laboratories/Lab1/Code/src.PexTests/TestProgram.cs
﻿using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using System.Text;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

namespace TestProgram
{
    public class Integer
    {
        private int value;

        public Integer(int value)
        {
            this.value = value;
        }

        public int GetValue()
        {
            return value;
        }
    }

    public class ArrayClass
    {
        private Integer[] arr;

        public ArrayClass(int size)
        {
            this.arr = new Integer[size];
        }

        public void SetIntegerAt(int index, int value)
        {
            this.arr[index] = new Integer(value);
        }

        public int GetValueAt(int index)
        {
            return this.arr[index].GetValue();
        }
    }

    public class TestClass
    {
        private ArrayClass arrayClass;

        public TestClass(int arraySize)
        {
            this.arrayClass = new ArrayClass(arraySize);
        }

        public void SetValueAt(int index, int value)
        {
            arrayClass.SetIntegerAt(index, value);
        }

        public int GetValueAt(int index)
        {
            return arrayClass.GetValueAt(index);
        }
    }
}

//Laboratories/Lab1/Code/src.PexTests/PexTests.cs
﻿using System;
using NUnit.Framework;
using PexAPIWrapper;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework.Settings;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework.Exceptions;
using TestProgram;

[TestFixture]
public class Tests
{
    [PexMethod]
    public void PexTest(int value, int index)
    {
        AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index >= 0 & index < 100);

        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("value", value);
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("index", index);

        var test = new TestClass(100);

        test.SetValueAt(index, value);

        int result = test.GetValueAt(index);

        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("result", result);

        Assert.AreEqual(value, result);
    }
}

//Laboratories/Lab1/README.md
# Laboratory 1

## Objective
The objective of this laboratory is to provide an introduction to Test Driven Development (TDD), and learn to write unit tests for C# using NUnit. 
In addition, we will experiment with PEX, an automated exploration tool, to generate and execute testing scenarios programmatically.

## Introduction
This laboratory covers the following:

1. Introduction to TDD and NUnit.
2. Practicing TDD with a simple project.
3. Introduction to PEX, automated exploratory testing tool.
4. Building test cases using PEX for a simple C# project.
5. Debugging programs using PEX 

## Project

You can work with a simple console project.

## Tasks
To complete this laboratory, you will need to:

1. Fork this repository.
2. Clone the repository to your computer.
3. Open the project in Visual Studio.
4. Follow the instructions in each part.

## Submission
Please create a pull request to submit your assignment.

**Disclaimer:** All the activities performed in the lab should be performed in your own computer to make it easier to troubleshoot during the exercises.
Remember, sharing codes is risky and it is better to learn through your mistakes.

Good Luck, and have fun with the lab.

## References

- https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/test/how-to-use-pex-annotations?view=vs-2019
- https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/test/unit-test-basics?view=vs-2019
- https://www.nunit.org/
- https://github.com/microsoft/Pex
